# Call-site ledger of validators 0.36.0 at e2e49fb3bb4b9f1ce955638386aa167356d24055.
# Hand-written from the source; the judgements are in validators.md.
#
# The scope rules A, B1-B5 and C are in macro-benchmark/README.md. validators
# requires Python 3.11 or later and declares CPython only. Every public
# validator is a plain function decorated with @validator (utils.py:45): the
# module name is bound to the closure validators.utils.validator.wrapper, so a
# call by that name calls the wrapper, and the wrapper calls the decorated
# function (utils.py:84, 91; validators.md, J1). The wrapper returns True or a
# ValidationError, whose __bool__ runs where the package tests such a result
# (J2). Arguments are client values of the annotated types (str, mostly).
default reject validators.utils.ValidationError.__bool__: the tested value is not a ValidationError; it is a str, bool, int, None, list, dict, set, re.Match, ipaddress or uuid object, a client value, or the result of a package function that is not a decorated validator (the truth sites that test the result of a call of a decorated validator accept the candidate explicitly)
default reject validators._extremes.AbsMax.__ge__, validators._extremes.AbsMin.__le__: the operands of this comparison are ints, strs or client values, never an AbsMax or AbsMin object: they are ints and strs that the function makes, or parameters annotated str, int or Union[str, int]; only the min_val and max_val of between are annotated to admit AbsMin and AbsMax (between.py:19-20), and its sites accept the candidates explicitly
default reject validators.utils.ValidationError.__repr__, validators.utils.ValidationError.__str__: no ValidationError is formatted, converted with str() or formatted by %; the operands are ints and strs (the left operand of every % is an int, so % is a modulo), and str(exp) converts a ValueError, TypeError or UnicodeError (ValidationError subclasses Exception only); only ValidationError.__str__ calls repr on a ValidationError (accepted there)

